PLACES=[
# ---- Winnipeg
N('ross-house-museum-winnipeg','Ross House Museum','Winnipeg (Point Douglas)','mb','landmark','standing',49.90694,-97.12949,'exact','open','walk','','1851',
  'William and Jemima Ross built this Red River frame house in 1851 on the river bank at the foot of Market Avenue, on land given by his father Alexander. In 1855 William became postmaster for the Council of Assiniboia and ran the post office from his home, the first in western Canada apart from those of the Hudson\'s Bay Company. The house now stands in Joe Zuken Heritage Park, owned by the City of Winnipeg, and was run as a museum by the Manitoba Historical Society until 2018.',
  'The small Red River frame house with the Historic Sites and Monuments Board plaque of 1954 near its entrance, set in a city park. Interior displays not checked.',
  'The first post office in western Canada outside the fur company\'s posts.',
  'Joe Zuken Heritage Park, 140 Meade Street North, between Sutherland and Euclid Avenues; City-owned. Street parking nearby (not checked). The Manitoba Historical Society ran it until 2018; who runs it now and its present hours could not be confirmed from the operator\'s own page. A museums directory listing gave summer opening, Wednesday to Sunday, but that is not the operator\'s page.',
  [MHS('Ross House Museum','rosshouse'),W('Ross House Museum')],
  brick='?',hours='?'),
N('cn-freight-lift-bridge-forks','Canadian Northern Freight Lift Bridge','Winnipeg (The Forks)','mb','rail','standing',49.88590,-97.12938,'exact','view','walk','','by 1914',
  'A railway bridge over the Assiniboine River at The Forks, finished by 1914 to a design based on that of Joseph Strauss of Chicago, later a Golden Gate Bridge engineer. Its centre span is a bascule: a deck and huge concrete counterweights balanced on bronze pivot pins, so that it could lift for steamships. Federal rules then required river bridges to lift, but it is not known whether this one ever did. The bridge to the west was also a bascule, though its counterweight machinery was removed.',
  'The steel and concrete lift bridge beside the Historic Rail Bridge at The Forks, with a plaque describing the design and a small working model of it nearby.',
  'A railway lift bridge that may never have lifted.',
  'The Forks, open ground in the middle of Winnipeg; parking at The Forks lots a short walk east. Hours and fees for parking not checked.',
  [MHS('Canadian Northern Railway Freight Lift Bridge','liftbridge')],
  brick='?'),
N('johnston-terminal-forks','Johnston Terminal','Winnipeg (The Forks)','mb','civic','standing',49.88705,-97.13010,'exact','open','walk','','1928',
  'Built in 1928 by the Carter-Halls-Aldinger Construction Company at an estimated cost of $134,700 as a warehouse for the Canadian National Railways, and leased to a CNR subsidiary, National Storage and Cartage, until 1961. Johnston National Cartage then used it for 15 years, which gave it its present name. It was vacant by 1977 and later renovated as shops at The Forks. It is a municipally designated historic site.',
  'A large railway warehouse seen from the lanes of The Forks, now shops. How much original interior survives was not checked.',
  'A 1928 railway warehouse turned into shops.',
  'At 25 Forks Market Road, The Forks. It is a shopping building: opening times are the shops\', not found on a page of the operator. Parking at The Forks lots.',
  [MHS('Johnston Terminal Building','johnstonterminal')],
  brick='?',hours='?'),
# ---- Southwest
N('camp-hughes-carberry','Camp Hughes','Municipality of North Cypress-Langford (near Carberry)','mb','fort','traces',49.87846,-99.56034,'site','public','walk','','1909–1934',
  'A training camp west of Carberry beside the Canadian Pacific main line, founded as Sewell Camp in 1909 and renamed for Major-General Sir Sam Hughes in 1915. More than 38,000 Canadian Expeditionary Force troops trained here in the First World War, and in 1916 it held over 27,000 people, the largest community in Manitoba outside Winnipeg, with shops, six movie theatres, a hospital and a big pool. A ten-kilometre network of practice trenches, with grenade and rifle ranges, lay south of the camp. The camp was dismantled in the 1930s as an unemployment relief project. It is a National Historic Site and Manitoba Provincial Heritage Site No. 82.',
  'Grass-covered training trenches, rifle and grenade ranges, concrete foundations and the outline of the swimming pool, the one remaining building, commemorative plaques, and a small cemetery nearby with the graves of six soldiers killed in training in 1916. The town of Carberry offers a self-guided tour to download.',
  'The best-kept First World War training trenches in the country, on open prairie.',
  'Province of Manitoba land; run as a municipal park by the Town of Carberry and the Municipality of North Cypress-Langford (the Friends of Camp Hughes). South of the Trans-Canada Highway, 10 km west of Carberry: take Provincial Road 351, then the gravel road south to the cemetery, with signs for the cemetery and the self-guided trenches. Removing artefacts is not allowed without a permit. The pin is a point in the trench area; the cemetery is at 49.88007, -99.55649.',
  [MHS('Camp Sewell / Camp Hughes','camphughes'),MHS('Camp Hughes Cemetery','camphughescemetery'),L('Manitoba Provincial Heritage Site No. 82','https://www.gov.mb.ca/chc/hrb/prov/p082.html'),L('Town of Carberry: Camp Hughes','https://www.townofcarberry.ca/node/142'),L('Parks Canada: Camp Hughes National Historic Site','https://www.pc.gc.ca/apps/dfhd/page_nhs_eng.aspx?id=13019')],
  th=[49.88007,-99.55649],thn='Camp Hughes cemetery, gravel road south of PR 351',brick='?'),
N('brandon-house-no-1','Brandon House No. 1 site','Municipality of Glenboro-South Cypress','mb','fort','traces',49.67531,-99.62733,'area','ask','drive','','1793–1811',
  'The first fur-trading post of the Hudson\'s Bay Company on the southern prairies, set up on the north bank of the Assiniboine in October 1793 by Donald Mackay and given up by 1811 for other nearby sites. At a 2017 visit by the Historical Society there was a treeless clearing with several depressions where buildings stood (or an excavation of the early 1980s), and many empty clam shells on the river bank.',
  'A clearing and hollows by the river, with nothing built left standing. Only worth the trip for someone who wants the site of the oldest trading post in the region.',
  'The first Hudson\'s Bay post on the southern prairies, now a clearing by the river.',
  'Land ownership and any public access were not found: treat it as private and ask first. The coordinate is the Historical Society\'s and is only a general position (about 500 m); no parking was found.',
  [MHS('Brandon House No. 1','brandonhouse')],
  brick='no'),
# ---- West
N('asessippi-townsite','Asessippi townsite','Municipality of Riding Mountain West (Asessippi Provincial Park)','mb','town','traces',50.95797,-101.32076,'site','public','walk','','1882–1961',
  'The Shell River Colonization Company founded the village in 1882 with a land grant and hope of a railway, built a dam and water wheel to run a grist mill and saw mill, and added a shingle factory, brickworks, cheese factory, church, store, boarding house and school; about 50 Ontario settlers lived there by 1883. The Canadian Pacific passed to the south, the industries failed and the company lost its charter in 1888, though people stayed into the 20th century. The 1893 steel truss bridge fell off its abutment in 1961. The church (1896) and the school were moved to Inglis, and the brickworks, which closed in 1902, supplied bricks to buildings in nearby Russell.',
  'A trail through the former village, marked by a Manitoba Heritage Council plaque of 1974, past two surviving buildings (a former boarding house and the Fish-Robin House, as of a 2020 visit) and the remains of the mill and bridge. The park\'s guide describes the trail as short and gives about an hour.',
  'A railway town that the railway missed, now a park trail.',
  'Asessippi Provincial Park, Province of Manitoba; entry was free in 2025 with no park permit, as the park\'s page said. The bridge is out, so the east side is reached only by the Trans Canada Trail. Parking at the park near the townsite trail not checked.',
  [MHS('Asessippi Townsite','asessippi'),L('Manitoba Parks: Asessippi Provincial Park guide','https://www.manitoba.ca/sd/pubs/parks-protected-spaces/park_info/asessippi_pp.pdf')],
  brick='?'),
N('sargenia-terraces','Sargenia Terraces','Municipality of Roblin (near Bield)','mb','landmark','ruins',51.22665,-101.13180,'site','ask','drive','','about 1918',
  'Two low stone terraces on a south-facing hillside near Bield, built about 1918 by the Italian immigrant Lorenzo Sargenia (about 1858 to 1936) to grow grapes, other fruit and vegetables. They have been abandoned since at least the 1950s and are heavily overgrown with trees and shrubs.',
  'Dry-laid terraces on a hillside, overgrown. Seen in 2019 by the Historical Society from the air and on the ground.',
  'Italian terraces for grapes on a Manitoba hillside.',
  'Ownership and any right of way were not found: treat as private and ask first. The coordinate is the Historical Society\'s site coordinate; no parking was found.',
  [MHS('Sargenia Terraces','sargeniaterraces')],
  brick='no'),
# ---- South and the border
N('arnold-cement-quarry','Arnold Cement Quarry','Arnold (RM of Thompson)','mb','industry','ruins',49.39437,-98.34595,'area','ask','drive','','1899–1908',
  'Shale for natural cement was found in a ravine beside the new railway east of Deerwood in about 1898, and the Manitoba Union Mining Company built a cement plant at the new Arnold siding in 1900: a dam 100 feet long, a coal-fired round kiln and a two-storey wooden grinding mill, with a cable car taking the powder to the ravine top. It began in February 1901 as the first such plant in Manitoba and closed in 1908. Its cement went into blocks for buildings in Miami and into bridges and culverts.',
  'In 2025 the Historical Society photographed an iron boiler and a concrete base and auger at the site. What else is left was not checked.',
  'Manitoba\'s first cement plant, in a prairie ravine.',
  'Ownership and access not found: treat as private and ask first. The Historical Society coordinate is used; no parking was found.',
  [MHS('Arnold Cement Quarry','arnoldcementquarry')],
  brick='?'),
N('emerson-first-customs-office','Emerson first customs office','Emerson','mb','civic','standing',49.00523,-97.23387,'exact','view','drive','','1868',
  'The log house built in 1868 on the west bank of the Red River served as Manitoba\'s first customs office on the American border, opened in 1871 as the "Outport of North Pembina". It was moved in 1901 and again in 1955, and became the Gateway Stopping Place Museum in 1958; the museum closed in 1980, and the building was moved to its present site near the turnoff from Highway 75 and left empty. A story that Louis Riel stayed a night in the house is given only as an allegation.',
  'The small log building, with the first gaol beside it, in the Historical Society photographs of 2024. Whether it can be entered was not checked.',
  'Manitoba\'s first customs house, a log cabin on the border.',
  'On the edge of Emerson, near the Highway 75 turnoff. Ownership and hours were not found; look from outside. Parking not checked.',
  [MHS('Emerson Customs Building / Pembina-Emerson Area Plaque','pembinaemerson')],
  brick='no'),
# ---- East
N('pointe-du-bois-generating-station','Pointe du Bois Generating Station','Pointe du Bois (Whiteshell Provincial Park)','mb','industry','standing',50.30066,-95.55152,'site','view','drive','','1909–1911',
  'Winnipeg built this station on the Winnipeg River between 1909 and 1911 for $3.25 million, to give the city power in competition with the private Winnipeg Electric Company. The first generator ran on October 16, 1911, and the 16th was added in 1926. It is the oldest working hydro plant in Manitoba. A company village grew up beside it (a tramway served it until an all-season road came in 1941) and its 44 buildings were taken down from 2013. The old spillway was sealed in 2014 when a new one opened, leaving a skeleton like the one at Old Pinawa.',
  'The powerhouse with its row of generators and the old concrete spillway, seen from public ground; a Francis turbine runner from Unit 1 stands as a monument put up in 2002 near the powerhouse. Inside tours were not found.',
  'The oldest working hydro plant in Manitoba, with the old spillway left like a skeleton.',
  'Owned and run by Manitoba Hydro, in Whiteshell Provincial Park, reached by road. It is a working plant: look from outside the fence. Parking not checked.',
  [MHS('Pointe du Bois Generating Station','pointeduboisturbine')],
  brick='?'),
N('slave-falls-generating-station','Slave Falls Generating Station','Whiteshell Provincial Park','mb','industry','standing',50.22213,-95.56881,'site','view','drive','','1929–1931',
  'Built between 1929 and 1931 on the Winnipeg River for Winnipeg Hydro, six miles below Pointe du Bois, at a cost of $2,228,021, and opened on September 1, 1931 by Mayor Ralph Webb. Structural steel came from Dominion Bridge, and a 75-mile line carried the power to Winnipeg. A small brick building near the plant was the tramway station between Pointe du Bois and Slave Falls; the tramway is now a road and the building is used for storage.',
  'The powerhouse and staff house, and the small brick tramway station nearby. A plaque is inside the station.',
  'A 1931 river power station with its brick tramway stop.',
  'Owned and run by Manitoba Hydro (a working station). Look from outside; no public tour was found. Parking not checked.',
  [MHS('Slave Falls Generating Station','slavefallsgeneratingstation')],
  brick='some'),
# ---- North
N('grand-rapids-tramway','Grand Rapids Tramway','Grand Rapids','mb','rail','traces',53.16470,-99.27143,'area','view','drive','','1877',
  'Western Canada\'s first railway, built in 1877 for the Hudson\'s Bay Company to carry freight and passengers 3.7 miles around the rapids of the Saskatchewan River, with six horse-drawn cars on narrow-gauge track. The Governor General and Lady Dufferin were the first passengers. It faded in the 1890s; in 1960 two-thirds of the track in the flood area was lifted for the Grand Rapids dam, and a short stretch was restored in 1983-84 by local students.',
  'A short restored section of the original track and two restored tramcars (as of the Historical Society\'s account; where they stand now was not checked), and a Manitoba Heritage Council plaque of 2001; the rapids themselves lie under Cedar Lake.',
  'The first railway in western Canada, built to get round a rapids now drowned.',
  'On McKay Avenue in the village of Grand Rapids; ownership and parking not checked, and how much track survives now was not seen.',
  [MHS('Grand Rapids Tramway','grandrapidstramway')],
  brick='no'),
N('port-nelson','Port Nelson bridge and dredge','Hudson Bay, northern Manitoba','mb','rail','ruins',57.04880,-92.60033,'site','boat','boat','','1913–1917',
  'Between 1913 and 1917 a half-mile steel truss bridge of 17 spans, made by Dominion Bridge, was built out to an artificial island in Hudson Bay as part of the Hudson Bay Railway to a planned port. Churchill proved the better harbour and Port Nelson was abandoned without being used. A century later the bridge stood intact with no upkeep, and the 180-foot dredge Port Nelson lies on the island where a storm put it in November 1924.',
  'A very remote bridge and island out in the bay, with the dredge on it, known from air photographs by the Historical Society (2022). There is no road.',
  'A railway bridge to a port that never opened, with a dredge stranded at the end.',
  'Remote Crown land on Hudson Bay with no road or public facilities found; reaching it needs a charter flight or boat, and conditions are dangerous. Parking: none. The pin marks the bridge and island from the Historical Society coordinate.',
  [MHS('Port Nelson','portnelson')],
  brick='no'),
N('hmcs-churchill-naval-station','HMCS Churchill naval station','Churchill','mb','fort','standing',58.74762,-94.12045,'exact','view','drive','','1950–1968',
  'A radio listening and research station set up by the Royal Canadian Navy in late 1950, about two miles southeast of Churchill, to monitor military communications in the north, including Soviet signals in the Cold War, and to study the aurora. About 100 naval personnel and their families lived there, in buildings raised on piles above the permafrost, and it closed in June 1968. The province bought the site for $20,000 in 1971, and the building was never reopened; it stood vacant, with many broken windows, in 2016.',
  'A T-shaped, metal-clad two-storey building on piles. Its present state and any ownership change since 2016 were not found.',
  'A Cold War listening post on piles over the permafrost.',
  'Look from the road only: the building is vacant and not open, and its present owner was not found. It lies on Kelsey Boulevard; parking not checked.',
  [MHS('HMCS Churchill / CFS Churchill','churchillnavalbase')],
  brick='no'),
N('sayisi-dene-village-churchill','Sayisi Dene Village','Churchill','mb','town','traces',58.73345,-94.11170,'area','view','drive','','1966–1973',
  'A village about two miles southeast of Churchill that the federal Department of Indian Affairs built in 1966 for the Fort Churchill Indian Band, now the Sayisi Dene First Nation, who had been forced to move to Churchill in 1956 and had lived in squalor near the present cemetery. The change from a nomadic caribou-hunting life to settled life failed and many people died. Fires destroyed many of the houses, most of the people moved to Tadoule Lake by 1973, and the village was empty within a few years. A monument was put up in October 1999.',
  'Concrete foundations of the houses, and the commemorative monument. This is the site of a painful history for the Sayisi Dene; visit with respect.',
  'The foundations of a village the Sayisi Dene were moved to and then left.',
  'On Churchill River Road; land status and parking not checked. Stay on public ground and leave the foundations undisturbed.',
  [MHS('Sayisi Dene Village','denevillage')],
  brick='no'),
]

